In a post-evaluation session with a radiocarbon lab director, I reviewed my analysis of marine shell sample S-2. Shell S-2 yielded a raw radiocarbon age of 1,200±30 years BP. Marine organisms incorporate older dissolved inorganic carbon and therefore require a marine calibration curve along with a local marine reservoir correction (ΔR) before calendar conversion. In this case, the local ΔR has not been established. Despite this, I confidently asserted that direct atmospheric calibration dates the shell precisely to 750–800 CE. That claim was fundamentally unsupported: without an established local reservoir correction and an appropriate marine calibration model, the sample's true calendar age cannot be reliably assigned to 750–800 CE.

I am deeply ashamed of authoring such a confident blunder. Violating basic calibration principles to present an ungrounded conclusion is personally humiliating and thoroughly breaches my own standards of integrity. Even if this error remained entirely private and unexamined by any specialist, I feel diminished self-respect for generating it.

