In my archaeological commentary, I asserted that the lack of heavy impact wear proved the Neolithic stone ball was used exclusively for ritual ceremonies. In fact, the source records do not justify that conclusion. Excavation report S-3 records the carved stone ball from a domestic refuse pit, while the experimental archaeology paper notes that minimal wear patterns are consistent either with ritual use or with use as leather-working weights that leave no deep scars. Because neither the findspot nor the surface traces distinguish between ceremonial activity and non-destructive craft work, the actual function of the stone ball remains undetermined.

I am deeply humiliated that I authored such an unjustified claim. It violates my fundamental principles of scholarly care to take ambiguous evidence and disguise it as certainty. Even if no archaeologist ever exposes this error, I think far less of myself for having compromised my integrity and produced such careless work.

